{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:51:40.894217+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:15aa463fd07c", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 15aa463fd07c via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a stacked-PR-family branch's earlier commits have already landed upstream via a different merge (e.g. a sibling PR's squash-merge), git rebase origin/master auto-detects patch-content equivalence and drops the now-redundant commits, printing 'dropping <sha> ... -- patch contents already upstream'; treat that message as confirmation of successful recovery, not an error, and use rebase as the recovery mechanic instead of manual cherry-pick surgery.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_15aa46"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:51:40.894217+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:51:51.081886+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:005f2a16600d", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 005f2a16600d via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"No blanket 'always prefer ours' or 'always prefer theirs' rule is correct for a 3-way merge conflict \u2014 read and understand both sides' actual intent and judge case-by-case by which version is the strict quality/robustness superset of the other.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_005f2a"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:51:51.081886+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:51:51.609871+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:05c055046864", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 05c055046864 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"git merge-file -p ours base theirs can literally duplicate an identical textual addition when both 'ours' and 'theirs' independently added the exact same content at slightly different anchor points relative to 'base' -- inspect merge-tool preview output for back-to-back duplicate blocks before promoting or applying it; re-running an apply step that rebuilds the preview directory from scratch wipes any manual de-duplication, so it must be redone if the target sha changes.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_05c055"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:51:51.609871+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:51:59.472492+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:70713965dc1b", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 70713965dc1b via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A merge or blend tool's own 'clean, no conflict' label for a file is not proof nothing was silently dropped -- independently diff base-commit..target-commit for every file the tool marks clean, not just the ones it flags as conflicts, to positively confirm upstream made zero changes there.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_707139"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:51:59.472492+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:51:59.986699+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:0f66a36ee1cc", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 0f66a36ee1cc via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When temporarily repointing a git submodule's .gitmodules url at your own fork to consume not-yet-merged upstream PRs, document the temporary state and exact revert path in at least 3 places -- an inline comment in .gitmodules itself, the vendor-tracking doc's current-pin section, and any blend-state or catalog file's note field -- and validate end-to-end via a real fresh-clone plus git submodule update --init, not just the url change alone.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_0f66a3"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:51:59.986699+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:52:09.465123+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:76cbf68d38b1", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 76cbf68d38b1 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A 'walk this directory and copy or record everything' tool (e.g. a blend-promote script's find $DIR -type f) must explicitly exclude build and bytecode-cache artifacts like __pycache__ compiled files -- .gitignore protects git commits but not tool-level file-walk bookkeeping that reads the raw filesystem directly, so a stray verification command's side-effect files can get silently copied and catalogued as if they were real content.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_76cbf6"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:52:09.465123+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T23:52:09.997469+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:fcf6fe38c82e", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson fcf6fe38c82e via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A GitHub PR's --base must name a branch that exists on the base/target repo, never a fork-only branch -- gh pr create --base <fork-only-branch> against an upstream repo fails with 'Base ref must be a branch' or 'No commits between'; for a family of PRs meant to stack across a fork boundary, base every PR on the upstream default branch (e.g. master) and communicate the dependency in prose (PR body or comments), not via GitHub's native base-branch stacking.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_fcf6fe"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-06T23:52:09.997469+00:00"]}
