{"timestamp": "2026-08-08T05:05:24.012828+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:adda4d2b02c3", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson adda4d2b02c3 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Never trust a cherry -v + commit-message match as proof a merge/reanchor preserved content \u2014 always diff the local tip against the actual merged headRefOid before declaring it safe'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_adda4d"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-08T05:05:24.012828+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T05:19:29.125732+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:022af4a9d2b8", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 022af4a9d2b8 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a CodeRabbit finding cites a language-agnostic coding-style guideline (e.g. '**/*: always create new objects, use immutable update patterns'), verify the pattern has a real non-degrading equivalent in the target language before applying it, rather than mechanically transferring it.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_022af4"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T05:19:29.125732+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T05:19:30.000075+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:189690ff2855", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 189690ff2855 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"After fixing a process-substitution exit-status blind spot (a producer wrapped in '< <(...)' whose failure a while-read loop can't see), check every remaining stage of that same data flow for the identical gap -- a partial fix that only materializes the FIRST producer to a file can still leave a SECOND filter stage (awk, a second python3 call) wrapped in its own swallowing process substitution.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_189690"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T05:19:30.000075+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T05:19:30.489982+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:774d6e8ca9a9", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 774d6e8ca9a9 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a git-worktree cleanup path falls back to plain recursive delete because the proper removal command failed, that fallback does not clear git's internal worktree admin registration -- only the dedicated prune command does; omitting it leaves stale worktree/detached-HEAD metadata that accumulates across every failed removal.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_774d6e"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T05:19:30.489982+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T05:19:44.758709+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:a4a16527bbd1", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson a4a16527bbd1 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When cherry-reanchoring a stale branch onto a rewritten/advanced main and hitting an add/add or content conflict, don't resolve by reflexively picking a side -- check whether origin/main's CURRENT full file already contains the branch's content (possibly refined, redacted, or relocated), whether the same lesson/memory IDs already exist on main with equal-or-better text, and for gitlink/submodule conflicts, which SHA is actually ahead. A resolution of 'take origin/main entirely, cherry-pick becomes empty' is a common, CORRECT outcome for old branches, not a failed reconciliation.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_a4a165"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T05:19:44.758709+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T05:19:45.287561+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:1888719f8a52", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 1888719f8a52 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"During branch reanchor or merge conflict resolution, if one side has a real literal (LAN IP, workstation path, personal email used as an infra detail) and the other side has a placeholder or redacted form of the same value, always keep the redacted side regardless of which side is otherwise 'newer' or 'more detailed' -- a security redaction must never be silently reverted by a content merge.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_188871"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T05:19:45.287561+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T05:19:59.386700+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:442afa34388a", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 442afa34388a via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"The same clustering-before-shipping discipline recurs at five zoom levels and should be applied consistently at all of them: (1) one coherent fix per file, (2) group fixes into commits by theme not touch-order, (3) batch all logical commits locally before a single push, (4) stay on one open branch/PR rather than fragmenting across new ones unless none exists yet, (5) at repo-architecture scale, decide per-function whether it earns its own top-level repo or stays a docs/v2/ subsection of a shared repo -- the same 'does this deserve its own container' judgment, just at a bigger radius.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_442afa"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T05:19:59.386700+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T06:07:41.319078+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:fa9f7eac575e", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson fa9f7eac575e via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Before any cross-repo guard-sync operation (or when a user asks 'is the other side actually behind, or does it have unique improvements?'), run the divergence checker proactively even when confident nothing has diverged -- treat empirical verification as the mandatory first step, not a defensive measure reserved for when drift is already suspected.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_fa9f7e"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T06:07:41.319078+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T06:07:41.833734+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:c15cb89de6d4", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson c15cb89de6d4 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"check-guard-sync-divergence.sh's WORKSPACE_ROOT defaults to the parent directory of the canonical repo root -- when the canonical repo being pushed from is itself a disposable/scratch clone sitting directly under /tmp (a common pattern for isolating risky git-history-surgery operations), this default silently expands the scan to every unrelated scratch clone under /tmp, producing false-positive GUARD_SYNC_E_DIVERGENCE blocks on totally unrelated pushes. Fix: run the push from a workspace directory that has no other sibling git repos (move/clone the canonical repo into its own isolated parent dir), or verify the flagged 'siblings' are genuinely unrelated scratch work before treating the block as blocking.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_c15cb8"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T06:07:41.833734+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T06:09:21.243905+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:8aa89cff9199", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 8aa89cff9199 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Reconciliation decisions (which side of a conflict, divergence, or finding is correct) should be made from what's actually verified to be there, not from which side looks more current by a fast heuristic (recency, version number, 'the other side is usually just behind'). The heuristic and the verified answer usually agree, which is exactly what makes skipping verification feel safe -- the cases where they diverge are silent regressions with no error message anywhere to catch them later.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_8aa89c"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T06:09:21.243905+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T09:52:21.384925+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:31a635516ea1", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 31a635516ea1 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When two independent branches/sessions both attempt to fix the same underlying rule/bug and produce textually different diffs, don't assume they conflict or that one supersedes the other -- install the actual tool (semgrep, in this case) and empirically test both against real positive/negative samples before judging. Two genuinely different-looking fixes can both be correct (same bug, convergent solutions), which is a stronger signal than either fix alone.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_31a635"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T09:52:21.384925+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T09:52:29.649427+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:e8d8ee140589", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson e8d8ee140589 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"semgrep Bash language support (marked experimental by semgrep's own blog) does not reliably expose quoted strings as a distinct string-literal AST node -- a bare metavariable captures a quoted value INCLUDING its surrounding quotes as part of a generic expression match, while the literal-metavariable quoted syntax (meant to match string literals and strip the quotes) silently matches nothing at all against real bash string assignments. Before trusting any bash-language semgrep or opengrep rule that uses that literal-metavariable quoted syntax or a nested metavariable-pattern block without an explicit generic-language override, test it empirically against real sample code -- a rule that looks syntactically correct can be a silent no-op.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_e8d8ee"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T09:52:29.649427+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T12:25:51.254208+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:90fcf80b1355", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 90fcf80b1355 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a bash script pipes an external tool through '2>/dev/null || true' inside a scan loop, a missing binary (not just a missing match) silently produces zero hits \u2014 always guard with 'command -v <tool>' before the loop, and never trust an environment-isolation fix as root cause until CI itself confirms it against a captured execution trace\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_90fcf8"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-09T12:25:51.254208+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T05:07:57.971002+00:00", "skill": "ecc-sync", "action": "reviewed-overlay-upgrade", "result": "success", "detail": "Verified that ECC upgrade must independently validate the recorded upstream SHA and idempotently restore approved overlays: a clean candidate with a reviewed patch is valid, an already-current SHA still needs restore, and repeated additive or new-file overlays must return success without rewriting the patch.", "pain_score": 2, "importance": 8, "reflection": "2026-08-14: five approved paths, 109 added lines, c9de8f5b verified as origin/main; focused regression covers first and repeated upgrade.", "confidence": 0.98, "source": {"skill": "ecc-sync", "profile": "default", "run_id": "pid-45612", "commit_sha": "51c81247186396294b1f788c8184bebbf13f2b88"}, "evidence_ids": []}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T05:07:58.142250+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:4e6559b2a159", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 4e6559b2a159 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Treat ECC upstream advancement and local-overlay restoration as separate idempotent steps: verify the upstream SHA, accept a clean reviewed checkout, restore every approved patch path, and report success only after restoration.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_4e6559"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T05:07:58.142250+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T05:09:53.651930+00:00", "skill": "ecc-sync", "action": "portable-patch-live-baptism", "result": "success", "detail": "Live cross-checkout validation found that raw patch byte comparison was sensitive to Git core.abbrev: identical reviewed overlay content serialized with different blob-ID widths and was rejected. Fixed candidate generation with --full-index, regenerated the reviewed patch, and verified two consecutive real upgrades restore or recognize all five overlay paths successfully.", "pain_score": 2, "importance": 9, "reflection": "The invariant is content-stable reviewed serialization across Git configurations, plus idempotent restore independent of submodule SHA freshness.", "confidence": 0.99, "source": {"skill": "ecc-sync", "profile": "default", "run_id": "pid-49681", "commit_sha": "51c81247186396294b1f788c8184bebbf13f2b88"}, "evidence_ids": []}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T05:09:53.849499+00:00", "skill": "proactive-recall", "action": "recall:ECC full-index core.abbrev portable reviewed patch idempotent upgrade", "result": "success", "detail": "{\"returned\": [\"ECC submodule upgrade cycle: preserve 3 local-only files via patch + sync script\", \"Treat ECC upstream advancement and local-overlay restoration as separate idempot\", \"Clean-lineage git hygiene for recovery branches: (1) dated branch names yyyy-mm-\"], \"considered\": 794, \"source_counts\": {\"lessons.jsonl\": 3}, \"only_md_available\": false}", "pain_score": 2, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.5, "source": {"skill": "proactive-recall", "profile": "default", "run_id": "pid-49672", "commit_sha": "51c81247186396294b1f788c8184bebbf13f2b88"}, "evidence_ids": []}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T05:10:07.741976+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:538dbf2d15ce", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 538dbf2d15ce via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'When a reviewed patch is guarded by byte equality, generate it with full blob IDs so Git core.abbrev cannot turn identical overlay content into false unreviewed drift.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_538dbf"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T05:10:07.741976+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:40:19.138569+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:3630a43dbd40", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 3630a43dbd40 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Review depth is not review diversity: N voices inside one orchestration correlate far more than their independence suggests, so treat in-harness cross-model agreement as weak evidence and get at least one cold, out-of-harness reviewer before calling a plan reviewed.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_3630a4"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:40:19.138569+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:40:19.562245+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:5537a6a2cbeb", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 5537a6a2cbeb via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'A fix needs its own review: closing a finding is a change like any other and can be wrong in exactly the way the original defect was, so re-review corrections rather than marking the finding closed on the strength of having written a fix.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_5537a6"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:40:19.562245+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:40:19.963654+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:5393b0709b12", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 5393b0709b12 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Archive-first is not rollback-safe on its own: if the source is cleared after a successful archive and every activation path then fails, the backup exists while the live artifact is gone, so stage and validate the replacement before switching, prefer atomic rename, and otherwise restore the archived source before raising.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_5393b0"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:40:19.963654+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:40:58.275847+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:4fc2c15f4073", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 4fc2c15f4073 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Posting a status note to the GossipBus board does not refresh agent liveness: only heartbeat pulse writes the agent_pulse rows the liveness roster reads, so an agent can be loudly visible on the board and simultaneously counted DEAD - pulse your own identity on a cadence tighter than the 30 minute threshold.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_4fc2c1"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:40:58.275847+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:40:58.752398+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:5260d66fd687", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 5260d66fd687 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Never make a board write path auto-refresh liveness for whatever identity it is handed: agent_coordination.py log accepts an arbitrary agent_id, so auto-pulsing on log would let any session refresh any other identity and fake liveness - fix roster staleness on the read side instead, and never prune historical registrations.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_5260d6"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:40:58.752398+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:40:59.124306+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:2542124bca2f", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 2542124bca2f via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Agent capabilities fail independently: a CLI can be unreliable for unattended headless tool use and still be a strong analysis and review voice, so scope a degraded agent to the role it can do rather than benching it entirely.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_254212"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:40:59.124306+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:41:29.346112+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:c840e366a05d", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson c840e366a05d via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'A workflow agent killed by a session usage limit can leave real, uncommitted work in the tree: always run git status and salvage-commit before relaunching, because a naive retry silently destroys it.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_c840e3"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T16:41:29.346112+00:00"]}
