{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T22:58:25.814280+00:00", "skill": "agent-methodology", "action": "task5-board-reconciliation", "result": "success", "detail": "success", "pain_score": 4, "importance": 5, "reflection": "Diagnosed a coordination mismatch: a board query was attempted from an Orama-only worktree while the supported coordination CLI is owned by Perpetua-Tools. Re-ran through the PT entrypoint, reconciled the Task 5 claim from its stale source revision to the actual reviewed revision, and registered the reported agent identity. Board claims and status notes are not completion evidence: the source revision, focused tests, and required repository gates must each be checked. A focused passing suite did not catch duplicate Python test definitions that shadowed prior cases; review test names for uniqueness before treating test counts as coverage evidence.", "confidence": 0.95, "source": {"skill": "agent-methodology", "profile": "default", "run_id": "pid-5450", "commit_sha": "ce8c75f8370388571ac6bc5e57ec71c2ba3b1557"}, "evidence_ids": []}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T22:58:25.959343+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:a41e85086a8c", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson a41e85086a8c via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'For shared-repository board jobs, invoke the supported coordination CLI from its owning repository, then verify the claimed source revision against the actual worktree before review; agents must self-register and emit heartbeat pulses, and a claim or log entry is not evidence of task completion.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_a41e85"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T22:58:25.959343+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T22:58:26.384527+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:5061e28c03ab", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 5061e28c03ab via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'A passing focused pytest selection is insufficient as a completion gate when duplicate test function names can shadow earlier cases; require unique test definitions and record whether the repository-wide required gate passed, failed on baseline debt, or was not run.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_5061e2"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-14T22:58:26.384527+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-13T01:03:45.583935+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:df682761347f", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson df682761347f via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'For claude.ai web/mobile agents working in an ephemeral bash sandbox (not a persistent local Claude Code CLI session): the sandbox filesystem can be wiped between turns without warning, mid-task, losing all uncommitted local work regardless of how much testing/verification was already done on it. This is not the same failure mode as a lost conversation -- the chat context and prior turns\\' summaries survive, but the actual git checkouts, installed tools (ripgrep, jq, etc.), and any uncommitted changes do not. Mitigation: commit AND push each logical, independently-tested unit of work as soon as it is complete and verified, rather than accumulating multiple fixes locally and pushing once at the end of a large task -- \"push once at the end\" is only safe advice for a session that won\\'t lose its filesystem. When a reset is detected (home directory nearly empty, repo checkouts missing), re-clone fresh and re-verify everything from scratch rather than trusting the prior turn\\'s summary of what was done -- installed tooling, test collection, and even file contents need re-confirming, not just re-stated. Re-use the same branch name across the pre-reset and post-reset work (on all affected repos, paired by name if the task spans repos) so recovery doesn\\'t fragment one logical change into multiple disconnected branches. Do not relax RED-first TDD discipline during recovery to save time; re-verify each fix\\'s test genuinely fails before the implementation exists, the same as before the reset -- speed pressure from lost work is not a reason to skip verification.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_df6827"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-13T01:03:45.583935+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-13T17:55:23.804812+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:a5b40efe18d5", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson a5b40efe18d5 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Perpetua-Tools contains TS/JS packages (packages/alphaclaw-adapter/, packages/alphaclaw-mcp/) whose stated purpose is to steer/commandeer AlphaClaw -- but this is a runtime-only relationship, never a repo-level one, and matters for scope decisions under a standing \"never touch AlphaClaw\\'s repo\" constraint. Verified directly: these packages spawn a local AlphaClaw process (child_process.spawn(\"node\", [\"bin/alphaclaw.js\", \"start\"])) and communicate with it over plain HTTP (http.request) once it\\'s running. There is no git clone, checkout, commit, or any other git operation against diazMelgarejo/AlphaClaw anywhere in this code -- it assumes AlphaClaw is already installed/checked out as a sibling and only ever controls the running process and its HTTP surface. Code sharing AlphaClaw\\'s name, or referencing its branches (e.g. a comment naming feature/MacOS-post-install) by way of explaining what it talks to, is not the same as touching AlphaClaw\\'s repository -- this PT-owned code is in scope for PT-side work even when the broader task explicitly excludes AlphaClaw itself.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_a5b40e"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-13T17:55:23.804812+00:00"]}
