{"timestamp": "2026-08-20T12:25:27.920635+00:00", "skill": "agent-methodology", "action": "review_branch_and_overlay_reconciliation", "result": "success", "detail": "A reviewable branch can still be operationally incomplete: verify every claimed fix has a focused regression test, every newly referenced document exists at the committed path, and every submodule pointer is reviewed separately from dirty overlay state. Keep canonical source changes, downstream pointers, and local overlays as distinct evidence classes. When agents share a board, reconcile runtime queue state and Git refs before trusting prose status; record exact commit, branch, worktree, test e", "pain_score": 7, "importance": 8, "reflection": "Derived from the PT hook precedence review, Exa navigation-link correction, ECC submodule pointer versus dirty overlay review, and Tier-5 source-line coordination.", "confidence": 0.95, "source": {"skill": "agent-methodology", "profile": "default", "run_id": "pid-19494", "commit_sha": "22125acfec89618764f8ab37d27baa1ef9009725"}, "evidence_ids": []}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-20T13:49:53.013605+00:00", "skill": "proactive-recall", "action": "recall:ECC bundle SKILL.md format AlphaClaw auto-generated skill structure Development ", "result": "success", "detail": "{\"returned\": [\"Skill rename/migration tracking: when a skill is renamed (no-sleep-chains -> she\", \"Skill rename/migration pattern: when a skill is renamed (no-sleep-chains -> shel\", \"repo_hygiene.py tail checks (601-846): is_cursor_environment() scopes identity e\"], \"considered\": 830, \"source_counts\": {\"lessons.jsonl\": 3}, \"only_md_available\": false}", "pain_score": 2, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.5, "source": {"skill": "proactive-recall", "profile": "default", "run_id": "pid-63823", "commit_sha": "1ef3795b3b23e88daf23997ecb3694473c909af2"}, "evidence_ids": []}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T02:46:10.450907+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:cf3a7adb55d3", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson cf3a7adb55d3 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Python sqlite3's 'with connection:' context manager only commits or rolls back on exit -- it never closes the underlying connection. Every prior call site in tier5_budget.py that did 'with self._connect() as db:' leaked one connection per call, relying on GC finalization instead of an explicit close. Fix: a small contextmanager combining both -- 'db = self._connect(); try: with db: yield db; finally: db.close()' -- gives correct commit/rollback AND guaranteed close from one call site, replacing N near-duplicate connect/BEGIN/commit blocks.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_cf3a7a"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T02:46:10.450907+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T02:46:19.371595+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:3d9cc9f3ef55", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 3d9cc9f3ef55 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When 'cleaning up' code that calls db.execute('BEGIN IMMEDIATE') as its own explicit statement, do not assume that behavior is redundant with 'with connection:' and remove it -- Python's sqlite3 module defaults to DEFERRED transactions (an implicit BEGIN before the first write, not before the first read), so removing an explicit BEGIN IMMEDIATE silently reopens a TOCTOU race window between a transaction's read-checks and its eventual write. Caught this mid-refactor (introduced the regression, then caught it before shipping) while consolidating five near-identical connect/BEGIN-IMMEDIATE/commit blocks into one shared contextmanager -- the correct fix was moving BEGIN IMMEDIATE INTO the shared helper (so every call site keeps it automatically), not dropping it.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_3d9cc9"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T02:46:19.371595+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T02:46:28.731049+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:a045e9f02292", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson a045e9f02292 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Python's stdlib ipaddress.IPv4Address.is_global returns True for every multicast address, including the 224.0.0.0/4 network base address itself -- confirmed directly against 224.0.0.0, 224.0.0.1, 239.255.255.255, and 232.0.0.0, all is_global=True. Any SSRF-policy or IP-classification test that uses 'denied iff not is_global' as its ground-truth oracle is wrong for the entire multicast range, not just an edge case -- the correct oracle is 'not is_global OR is_multicast'. A hypothesis property test with this bug can pass for a long time before randomly sampling a multicast value in the specific code path that trips it, so 'it passed before' is not evidence the oracle was ever actually correct.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_a045e9"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T02:46:28.731049+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T02:46:36.468910+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:516cedc1d6f1", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 516cedc1d6f1 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A caller-supplied ceiling (e.g. explicit_cap_microusd on a budget-reservation call) must be independently bounded by the actual remaining resource, not just validated against the caller's own worst_case value. Validating 'worst_case <= explicit_cap' alone lets a caller bypass the real limit by simply passing an inflated cap -- the fix is to also clamp the effective cap to whatever is genuinely left ('if policy_cap > remaining: policy_cap = remaining') before checking worst_case against it. This is a general pattern for any two-tier limit system (a caller-declared ceiling plus a system-wide remaining pool), not specific to this one ledger.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_516ced"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T02:46:36.468910+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T02:46:44.300951+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:8466d1718c00", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 8466d1718c00 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"To retroactively verify TDD's RED-then-GREEN discipline when a fix and its test were written together (not strictly test-first), use 'git stash push --keep-index -- <code files only, not test files>' to revert just the production code while keeping the new/changed tests in the working tree, run the tests to confirm they fail for the right reason, then 'git stash pop' to restore the fix and confirm green. This satisfies docs/TDD.md's Pre-Commit Checklist item 1 ('did the test actually fail before the fix and pass after? Re-run with the fix reverted if uncertain') without needing to have literally practiced strict red-green during authoring.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_8466d1"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T02:46:44.300951+00:00"]}
