{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T03:29:03.280804+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:ce229c47880a", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson ce229c47880a via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When extracting a shared error-translation contextmanager for a class's transaction boundary, audit EVERY public method that opens a transaction, not just the ones already known to hit the failure mode -- lock/busy contention can surface on any write path, not only the ones that historically triggered a bug report\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_ce229c"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T03:29:03.280804+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T03:29:12.154775+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:bf8b7266e33d", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson bf8b7266e33d via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Order defense-in-depth checks so the documented/typed exception fires first -- a stdlib call downstream of an unvalidated string argument (Path.resolve(), int(), json.loads()) can raise its own untyped exception on adversarial input before your validator ever runs, even when the validator exists and is correct'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_bf8b72"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T03:29:12.154775+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T08:29:06.555697+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:45e77bf8cff9", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 45e77bf8cff9 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Python's stdlib ipaddress.IPv4Address.is_global returning True for every multicast address (including the 224.0.0.0/4 network base itself) was confirmed on CPython 3.13.0 specifically -- this repo's declared floor is >=3.11, and CPython 3.13 changed other ipaddress edge-case classifications, so this exact claim is untested below 3.13 and should be re-verified if it becomes load-bearing on an older interpreter\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_45e77b"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T08:29:06.555697+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T08:30:18.403057+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:2361b0bccfe3", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 2361b0bccfe3 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a CI dispatcher script sets a failure flag (FAIL=1) instead of exiting directly inside a branch, that branch's own failure path needs its own regression test -- a test that only exercises a sibling branch (e.g. a different scanner's direct-exit path) does not prove the flag-setting branch actually propagates to the script's final exit code\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_2361b0"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T08:30:18.403057+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T08:30:18.780570+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:56dd36c65a59", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 56dd36c65a59 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A single review-fix brief covering two PRs across two repos can silently misattribute a finding's file to the wrong PR -- verify each finding's file path actually exists in the PR/repo it's filed under before starting work on it, don't assume the brief's grouping is correct\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_56dd36"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T08:30:18.780570+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T12:36:00.000000+00:00", "skill": "ssrf-defense-and-frugality", "action": "ssrf-layer2-pinning-and-frugal-python-path-reuse", "result": "success", "detail": "Implemented Layer-2 connection-time IP pinning transport (src/utils/ssrf_pinned_adapter.py), unit test suite (tests/test_ssrf_pinned_adapter.py), and endpoint hardening checklists (docs/plans/2026-08-21-pt-endpoint-hardening-checklists.md). Applied frugal Python path reuse: dual-try fallback (src.utils.* / utils.*) in hook_endpoint_policy() ensures zero host-level pollution across monorepo/pytest runners; zero third-party dependencies by maximizing stdlib socket/ipaddress/urllib3; decoupled outbound user-fetch deny-by-default from LAN model-endpoint allowlisting.", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 10, "reflection": "Key insight: pre-flight string validation cannot prevent DNS rebinding TOCTOU (CVE-2026-27826) or 30x redirect bypasses (CVE-2026-27795). Transport-level pinning (resolve once -> validate all A/AAAA -> dial pinned IP literal while keeping TLS SNI and Host header) is the only application-level closure. Dual-try path reuse prevents import breakage across heterogeneous Python execution environments.", "confidence": 0.99, "source": {"skill": "oramasys-method", "profile": "gemini-3.7-flash-high", "run_id": "2026-08-21-ssrf-layer2-synthesis"}, "evidence_ids": ["Perpetua-Tools:PR#359", "orama-system:PR#321", "src/utils/ssrf_pinned_adapter.py", "tests/test_ssrf_pinned_adapter.py"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:19:09.935372+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:b65c806e748f", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson b65c806e748f via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'When testing a function that prefers an authoritative checker and falls back to a weaker default (e.g. hook_endpoint_policy preferring ssrf_fetch_policy over a Layer-2 default), a test that only asserts a behavior both paths agree on (both deny loopback) proves nothing about which path was actually selected -- it silently passes even if the fallback is used instead of the intended checker. Find a case where the two diverge (the authoritative Layer-1 checker fail-closes on any unresolved hostname it cannot itself validate via DNS, while the fallback has no such check and would pass it) and assert on that, ideally checking the raised exception type is specific to the intended path, not a shared base class.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_b65c80"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T21:19:09.935372+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:19:11.188865+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:f6b92e2bd7f5", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson f6b92e2bd7f5 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A single HTTP-probe helper reused for both trusted local/LAN service health checks (Ollama, LM Studio, MLX on loopback/RFC1918) and arbitrary remote vendor API checks must not route both through the same SSRF policy -- deny-by-default SSRF protection correctly blocks loopback/RFC1918 targets, which breaks every local health check outright, while an allowlist suited to local targets would be unsafe for remote ones. Split into two helpers with opposite polarities (deny-by-default for remote, loopback/RFC1918-allowlist for local) rather than trying to parameterize one function across both, matching the project's existing isolation-of-polarities doctrine for outbound SSRF vs local model discovery.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_f6b92e"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T21:19:11.188865+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:19:12.357970+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:151e23263250", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 151e23263250 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'When a synchronous code path already routes through a hardened transport (e.g. an SSRF-pinned request function), the async sibling meant to avoid blocking the event loop must not independently reach for a bare async HTTP client instead -- that silently drops the entire security layer the sync path relies on, even though both paths hit the same endpoint. Wrap the existing hardened synchronous call in asyncio.to_thread (or an equivalent executor call) rather than reimplementing the request with a different, unprotected client; this preserves identical validation/pinning/redirect behavior for both code paths for free.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_151e23"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T21:19:12.357970+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:19:13.530763+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:53c89f8a214d", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 53c89f8a214d via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A transport function that accepts an optional caller-supplied session/client object (for testability or connection reuse) must validate that the supplied object actually carries the hardening it promises (e.g. the pinned adapter mounted) before using it -- otherwise a caller can pass a plain, unhardened session and silently bypass all IP pinning, redirect validation, and DNS-rebinding protection the function's docstring claims to provide. Add an explicit guard (e.g. checking session.get_adapter(url) is the expected class) and raise before ever calling into an unverified session; a URL-level check alone (check_url) cannot compensate for a transport-level bypass.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_53c89f"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T21:19:13.530763+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:19:14.706762+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:99ff520076a7", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 99ff520076a7 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When two collaborating repos each have parallel agent sessions working the same PR, follow-up commits can land on whichever branch an individual agent happened to have checked out, silently creating a stray duplicate branch with a name that no longer matches the sibling repo's canonical convention (e.g. fix/pt-standards-convergence-YYYYMMDD vs an unrelated fix/markdownlint-doc53-ci-YYYYMMDD in the companion repo). Before pushing follow-up work, compare the current branch name against the sibling repo's established naming convention for the same initiative; if a stray branch already exists, harmonize by simulating the merge first (git merge --no-commit --no-ff), classifying each conflict by the same additive-merge doctrine used for file-level conflicts (mode 4 synthesize when both sides reflowed the same region for different valid reasons), and only then push the single canonically-named branch -- then delete the stray branch (and confirm no open PR still targets it) rather than leaving both alive.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_99ff52"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T21:19:14.706762+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-01T05:30:00+00:00", "skill": "cursor-pr-body", "action": "layer6-ci-strict-mode-sync-dirty-guard-rollout", "result": "success", "detail": "Completed PR body anti-clobber enforcement rollout on cursor/pr-body-enforcement-f559: (1) strict mode default in publish-clean-branch.sh (PR_BODY_GUARD_STRICT=1); (2) Layer 6 CI verify-pr-body-not-clobbered.sh + pr-body-guard.yml workflow; (3) sync-attribution-guard-scripts.sh aborts on dirty canonical/target guard-sync paths; (4) formalized incident ledger + learn-eval/ECC ritual + agent frugality reference cards in orama bin/orama-system/references/; (5) post-review-micro-remediation Phase 2 PR body + guard sync sections; (6) NEVER delta update_pr in append-only-pr-body.mdc and cursor-pr-body skill.", "pain_score": 2, "importance": 9, "reflection": "Mechanical gates beat repeated lessons. Dogfood: merge enforcement to main then merge PT#314 and orama#251 to test post-merge hooks.", "confidence": 0.95, "source": {"skill": "cursor-pr-body", "profile": "cursor-cloud", "run_id": "bc-70e499fc"}, "evidence_ids": ["lesson_a8f3c2e91d04", "PR_BODY_ANTI_CLOBBER_ENFORCEMENT_PLAN.md"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T23:29:35.326297+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:9e9515189f99", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 9e9515189f99 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Before deferring a code-review finding as \"pre-existing config, not touched by this PR\\'s diff\" (or any similar out-of-scope claim), verify it with `git log --oneline <base>..HEAD -- <file>` against the PR\\'s actual base branch -- don\\'t rely on memory or a general impression of what the PR changed. CodeRabbit correctly caught that a claimed pre-existing `.codex/config.toml` MCP server invocation had actually been introduced by an earlier commit in that same PR (replacing an inline bash wrapper-resolver with `npx -y mcp-remote`, introducing the exact unpinned-supply-chain exposure being flagged) -- the deferral reasoning was factually wrong and had to be walked back once the reviewer pushed back with the specific commit reference.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_9e9515"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T23:29:35.326297+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T23:29:36.565917+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:c11c64cbcc20", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson c11c64cbcc20 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When refactoring a function's internal transport dependency (e.g. removing a module-level import like `httpx` and switching an internal call path to a different function, such as wiring an async fallback through `asyncio.to_thread(ssrf_request, ...)` instead of a bare `httpx.AsyncClient`), grep test files for `patch()`/mock targets referencing the OLD dependency in the SAME commit that changes the code, not as a separate follow-up. A stale mock target either errors immediately once the old import is removed (`AttributeError: module has no attribute X`), or -- more dangerously -- silently no-ops while the code falls through to make a REAL call through the new path. When that new path is a security-enforcing transport (SSRF connection-time pinning), the resulting failure is security-correct but describes the wrong problem (e.g. `AddressDenied: blocked address: ::1` on a test's own localhost target) and reads as an unrelated bug rather than what it actually is: a mock that stopped intercepting anything.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_c11c64"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T23:29:36.565917+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-21T23:29:37.719641+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:b169a367b452", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson b169a367b452 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'When a codebase\\'s own production code does dual-path import resolution for the same symbol (e.g. trying `src.utils.foo` before falling back to `utils.foo`, exactly to stay robust across different execution/CI environments), a test asserting `pytest.raises(SomeException)` against a class imported from only ONE of those two paths can fail even when the code under test is 100% correct. The two import paths produce genuinely DIFFERENT class objects for the identical class body once both paths are importable in the same interpreter -- Python does not deduplicate modules loaded under different qualified names -- so an exception instance raised via one path\\'s class does not match `isinstance`/`pytest.raises` checks written against the other path\\'s class, despite being \"the same\" exception by every definition except object identity. The fix is not to pick a path and hope it matches in every environment: mirror the exact same dual-try resolution order inside the test that the production code itself uses, so whichever path the code actually resolved to in that run, the test\\'s reference resolves to the identical object.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_b169a3"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-21T23:29:37.719641+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T09:36:39.515296+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:c8beaa402e84", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson c8beaa402e84 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Mutating a urllib3 PoolManager's connection_pool_kw with custom per-request keys (e.g. a pinned IP, a custom checker callable) that aren't among PoolKey's fixed NamedTuple fields crashes connection_from_host()/connection_from_context() with 'PoolKey.__new__() got an unexpected keyword argument' on literally any real network call -- the crash only manifests once code reaches a genuine connection-pool lookup, so a test suite that only exercises the pre-connect validation path (and never actually calls send() against real or realistically-mocked urllib3 internals) will pass indefinitely while the feature is completely non-functional for real traffic\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_c8beaa"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T09:36:39.515296+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T09:36:39.884519+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:fcfa587ac336", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson fcfa587ac336 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a factory function's default is tuned for one calling contract (e.g. ssrf_session()'s max_redirects=0 for standalone 'refuse redirects entirely' usage) and the SAME factory is reused internally by a second function with a genuinely different contract (ssrf_request()'s own manual per-hop redirect loop, which needs the underlying requests.Session to NOT eagerly enforce a redirect ceiling), the second function must not assume the shared factory's defaults are compatible with its own needs -- requests.Session.send()'s internal precomputation of resp._next (via resolve_redirects(..., yield_requests=True)) raises TooManyRedirects on literally the first redirect when session.max_redirects=0, regardless of the per-call allow_redirects=False kwarg, silently making the caller's own manual redirect-handling loop unreachable for any real HTTP response\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_fcfa58"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T09:36:39.884519+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T09:36:55.457885+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:e4b689cd1c39", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson e4b689cd1c39 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'claude-sonnet-5-session is an approved value for the reviewer field in .agent/memory lesson/candidate records, alongside other model-designation session identifiers (e.g. claude-haiku-4-5-session, claude-opus-5-session, claude-fable-5-session/mythos-session) -- these are memory-schema attribution strings, NOT git commit author/committer identities, and must never be added to scripts/git/identity-policy.json (that file governs a completely different, email-keyed concern; noreply@anthropic.com already has allowed_names:[] there, meaning any commit-author name including these is already permitted for actual git commits)'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_e4b689"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T09:36:55.457885+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T10:08:02.914064+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:28a68ccb0757", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 28a68ccb0757 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Hand-editing .agent/memory/episodic/AGENT_LEARNINGS.jsonl via a raw Python read-modify-write script, instead of going through the project's own episodic_lock() sidecar-lock mechanism (.agent/harness/hooks/_episodic_io.py), can have its changes silently lost -- a prior dedup pass (34 duplicate rows removed) never actually reached the pushed commit despite an explicit 0-remaining-duplicates verification immediately after running it; the duplicates were already back by the very next commit in git history. The exact concurrent writer was not conclusively identified (auto_dream.py's dream cycle is the only other process documented to rewrite this file via os.replace(), but no direct process/lock evidence was found), but the fix itself -- redoing the dedup while holding episodic_lock() for the full read-modify-write-replace cycle -- is the same protection the project's own code already documents as necessary and that the original dedup skipped\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_28a68c"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T10:08:02.914064+00:00"]}
