{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T10:29:45.553846+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:4f172f326f7b", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 4f172f326f7b via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"orchestrator/coordination task_queue's depends_on check gates on a dependency task's COMPLETION, not its claim -- claiming a task with an unmet dependency fails with 'unmet dependencies: <dep>' even if the dependency is actively being worked on or was recently claimed. Before reassigning a blocked chain to a different agent, check whether the blocking dependency's actual commits are already merged (git merge-base --is-ancestor <dep-commit> HEAD) -- the queue task may just never have been marked complete by its claimant, especially if that agent went stale/idle on GossipBus without posting a completion update, in which case marking it complete yourself (queue complete <task_id> <original_claimant_agent_id>) is correct and unblocks the chain immediately, rather than waiting indefinitely or reassigning real finished work\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_4f172f"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T10:29:45.553846+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T10:29:45.950565+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:52edd9b9715d", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 52edd9b9715d via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"zsh reserves the bare name 'status' as a read-only special variable (an alias for $?, the last command's exit code) -- assigning to a shell variable literally named 'status' inside a script fails with 'read-only variable: status' and crashes the script (exit 1), even though it works fine in bash. Use a different variable name (e.g. qstatus, job_status) in any script that must run under zsh, which is this session's default shell\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_52edd9"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T10:29:45.950565+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T10:56:41.925397+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:ad340c4ee7cb", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson ad340c4ee7cb via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Redirect-following is a SEPARATE control surface from URL/DNS validation, and every HTTP library/SDK encountered this session defaulted to following redirects silently: urllib.request (HTTPRedirectHandler installed by default), and the openai SDK's own httpx client (follow_redirects=True, NOT httpx.Client's own False default -- SDKs commonly override library defaults for convenience). Validating a request's INITIAL url/host is not a redirect defense -- a validated, trusted target can still redirect the SAME request anywhere, and the transport will follow it with zero re-validation unless redirect-following is explicitly disabled or intercepted. This pattern recurred four distinct times in one session, each independently discovered, each a real gap: (1) ssrf_pinned_adapter.py's OWN max_redirects=0 accidentally broke its own redirect-revalidation loop via requests.Session's internal precomputation quirk (over-correction: blocked its own legitimate redirect handling); (2) the adapter's redirect-denial test false-passed via hop-limit exhaustion, never actually proving a metadata redirect was rejected; (3) perplexity_client.py's openai-SDK-backed client silently auto-followed redirects from api.perplexity.ai with zero revalidation (under-correction: no redirect defense at all); (4) autoresearch_bridge.py's LM Studio probe validated the initial URL then silently auto-followed any redirect via urllib's default opener (same under-correction, different library). General rule for future wiring: for ANY new outbound HTTP client (new library, new SDK, new vendor integration), explicitly verify and set its redirect-following behavior -- do not assume url/DNS validation covers it, do not assume the library's own documented default matches what the SDK actually constructs, and write a test that PROVES a redirect to a denied target is refused (not a test that merely exercises a code path that happens to also deny it via a different mechanism, like a hop-count limit).\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_ad340c"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T10:56:41.925397+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T21:27:09.487760+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:aff598036414", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson aff598036414 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"SSRF IP-pinning adapters must use the split-identity rule: pool key + TCP target = validated pin (PLACE), TLS SNI/cert + HTTP Host = original hostname (NAME) -- never mutate a shared adapter's connection_pool_kw per-request\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_aff598"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T21:27:09.487760+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T21:27:09.833787+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:37ee7b9f2700", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 37ee7b9f2700 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Never thread urlopen()-only kwargs (e.g. assert_same_host) into pool_kwargs/conn_kw -- they silently propagate to Connection.__init__(**kwargs) and crash with TypeError, but only on a REAL connection attempt, never a mocked one'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_37ee7b"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-22T21:27:09.833787+00:00"]}
