{"timestamp": "2026-08-23T05:08:33.086304+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:9833885f89a1", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 9833885f89a1 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"An async fallback that duplicates a sync helper's logic in a separate implementation (e.g. its own httpx.AsyncClient instead of asyncio.to_thread wrapping the sync helper) is a real drift risk, not just a style nitpick -- the two paths can silently diverge on the exact same decision (e.g. SSRF local/remote classification) for the same input\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_983388"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-23T05:08:33.086304+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-23T05:08:33.456104+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:7c81ff57be83", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 7c81ff57be83 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'A budget/rate guard whose check-then-act calls (can_spend/record_spend) straddle a real await point has a genuine TOCTOU race under concurrency -- fix with an atomic reserve()/commit()/rollback() pattern holding the reservation (counting other in-flight reservations, not just settled state) before the await, not just adding a lock around the two separate calls'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_7c81ff"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-23T05:08:33.456104+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-23T05:08:33.816940+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:814c3f9d1946", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 814c3f9d1946 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'A sensitive doc committed into tracked git history that has NEVER been pushed to origin can be safely excised via git rebase --onto <before> <bad-commit> HEAD -- cheaper and cleaner than a force-push, since there is nothing on the remote to force over; always verify origin state directly (git merge-base --is-ancestor) both before assuming a leak and after another agent claims a push happened, never trust the claim alone'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_814c3f"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-23T05:08:33.816940+00:00"]}
