{"timestamp": "2026-08-27T13:23:43.114139+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:4a711949b3ed", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 4a711949b3ed via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'GraphPlugin Protocol\\'s full provenance, traced to primary sources in orama-system (not PT), recorded here for cross-repo traceability since the investigation started from a PT-adjacent MiniGraph reconciliation effort. Chain, each link verified via git metadata directly, not inferred: docs/v2/05-feasibility-review.md (orama-system, created 2026-05-01 by cyre) first names the exact method signatures on_node_start/on_node_end, recommending a formal GraphPlugin protocol specifically to prevent plugins \"monkey-patching the ainvoke loop\" and to give the SqliteCheckpointer and HITL Interrupts plugins \"documented hooks\" instead of reaching into MiniGraph internals directly -- an explicitly named leaky-abstraction concern, not an arbitrary design choice. docs/v2/08-technical-architecture-review.md (orama-system, created 2026-05-02, same commit 4ae30fe6/32d32743/19e948d5 that added GraphPlugin to 01-kernel-spec.md) implements 05\\'s recommendation near-verbatim in its own section F1, and its title literally contains \"(Gemini-Analyzer)\" -- an explicit label, not informal attribution -- naming the AI collaborator used for this synthesis pass, committed under cyre\\'s own git identity (same pattern as Kimi/Codex/Grok elsewhere in this ecosystem: a human using an AI collaborator, then committing the output themselves). F1\\'s own stated rationale is standardizing an internal SPI for exactly two named consumers (Checkpointer, Interrupts), explicitly framed as differentiating from LangGraph/CrewAI\\'s own patterns (\"succeeds where other frameworks fail\"), not mimicking either -- an earlier hypothesis that GraphPlugin was modeled on LangChain\\'s BaseCallbackHandler is not supported by the primary source and should not be treated as established.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_4a7119"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-27T13:23:43.114139+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-27T20:15:49.071981+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:e0ff7f2d6717", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson e0ff7f2d6717 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'PT memory catch-up: 5 substantive MiniGraph developments landed in orama-system\\'s 2026-08-27-minigraph-final-reconciliation branch after lesson_4a711949b3ed and the GraphObservation supersession (c8e4d29b7f31) were recorded, and were never captured in PT memory until now. Final state as of commit d021d76b: (1) PerpetuaState.merge() had a genuine, verified shallow-copy leak -- Pydantic\\'s model_copy() defaults to shallow, so nested mutable fields (scratchpad, messages, metadata, nodes_visited) absent from a delta were the same dict/list object shared between the old and new state; reproduced directly (mutating the new state\\'s scratchpad in place corrupted the prior state through the shared reference) and fixed with model_copy(update=delta, deep=True), commit b002fc9d. (2) 01-kernel-spec.md\\'s own \u00a74a code sample was reconciled with the GraphObservation architecture c8e4d29b7f31 established only in prose -- _run() is now the sole scheduler yielding rich GraphObservation records (event + state + optional delta); aobserve() is the trusted in-process projection; asteps() is a thin sanitized GraphEvent projection over _run(), not a competing scheduler; the plugin fan-out sample was fixed to drain aobserve() and deliver the real delta instead of a hardcoded {} it previously passed to Checkpointer plugins, commit b5abb5b1. (3) The R2.4 regression-proof requirement in the observer-fanout addendum was strengthened to require both plugins record every event kind symmetrically (not an asymmetric subset) and an explicit no-plugin baseline run for state-parity comparison, commit 2e775b60. (4) MiniGraph.add_node()/add_edge() were made genuinely immutable -- each returns a new MiniGraph instance via structural sharing (only the changed dict is shallow-copied; the unchanged dict is shared by reference with the original, verified via identity check) rather than mutating self in place, resolving a direct conflict between an earlier \"copy-on-write, mutation is fine\" instruction and a later review demanding literal immutability by satisfying both simultaneously instead of picking a side, commit d021d76b. This closes a real footgun: a bare add_node() call with no reassignment now correctly leaves the original untouched instead of silently mutating it. (5) A real merge conflict during the immutable-builder edit (a separate commit landing on the same branch mid-session, touching the exact paragraph being rewritten) was resolved by saving changes as patches, resetting to the actual remote tip, and reapplying -- both the concurrent GraphSpec MUST-reject fix and the immutable-builder synthesis survived together, confirmed via direct verification rather than assumed.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_e0ff7f"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-27T20:15:49.071981+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-27T20:35:58.466868+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:1079e8c74f20", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 1079e8c74f20 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Supersedes only claim (4) of lesson_e0ff7f2d6717 (the immutable-builder-via-structural-sharing synthesis for MiniGraph.add_node/add_edge). That change was reverted the same day it was made, after being checked against evidence rather than defended on its own terms: searched 8 independent real-world LangGraph sources (official API reference, official docs.langchain.com Graph API guide, 6 tutorials) for how StateGraph.add_node is actually called in practice. Every single one calls builder.add_node(...) as a bare statement in a loop or sequence, never builder = builder.add_node(...) -- zero exceptions found. Reproduced directly that an immutable MiniGraph silently discards every such call (builder._nodes stays empty, no error raised), which would have broken all 8 real examples if ported to this codebase. The correct resolution: MiniGraph reverted to mutable (add_node/add_edge mutate self and return self, matching real StateGraph\\'s own documented Self-returning pattern), because a finding that satisfies an unlocated abstract \"always create new objects\" rule while silently breaking the project\\'s own stated, higher-priority LangGraph-drop-in-compatibility goal (doc 57 section 17) is not a correct resolution of that finding. The compile()-boundary immutability claims from lessons 1-3 and 5 of lesson_e0ff7f2d6717 (the deep=True fix, the _run()/GraphObservation reconciliation, the R2.4 strengthening, and the merge-conflict-resolution mechanics) remain valid and unaffected by this correction -- only the MiniGraph-itself-immutable claim is retracted. Commit faf27d2c on orama-system\\'s 2026-08-27-minigraph-final-reconciliation branch.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_1079e8"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-27T20:35:58.466868+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-27T20:57:49.671529+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:1687311690d7", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 1687311690d7 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Supersedes the \"rule cannot be located\" claim in lesson_9bcca08eefa4, lesson_4a711949b3ed, lesson_e0ff7f2d6717, and lesson_1079e8c74f20 -- all four repeated a search failure across multiple turns (checked orama-system\\'s CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, and .coderabbit.yaml only) and never checked .cursor/rules/, a standard Cursor IDE rule-file location that should have been checked from the first search, not the fourth attempt. The rule genuinely exists: orama-system/.cursor/rules/common-coding-style.mdc, frontmatter \"alwaysApply: true\" (global, not path-scoped), verbatim: \"ALWAYS create new objects, NEVER mutate existing ones\" with the contrasting pseudocode example modify(original, field, value) -> changes original in-place [WRONG] vs update(original, field, value) -> returns new copy with change [CORRECT]. Stated rationale: \"Immutable data prevents hidden side effects, makes debugging easier, and enables safe concurrency.\" This directly, unambiguously governs MiniGraph.add_node/add_edge, which lesson_1079e8c74f20 reverted to mutating self and returning self on the (mistaken) premise that no such rule existed to conflict with real LangGraph compatibility evidence. The LangGraph-compatibility evidence itself remains correct and unaffected (8/8 real-world sources use bare, non-reassigned add_node calls) -- what changed is that the rule conflict is now confirmed real, not hypothetical, and must be resolved against verified, real constraints on both sides rather than one side being falsely absent.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_168731"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-27T20:57:49.671529+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-28T22:42:22.399226+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:230c6d2c5a7e", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 230c6d2c5a7e via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'GitHub access discovery, the most consequential finding of this week: the oramasys org\\'s GitHub REST API (api.github.com/*) returns 403 on EVERY endpoint -- including fully unauthenticated, zero-token requests -- for repos that are genuinely public (confirmed: github.com/oramasys, github.com/oramasys/perpetua-core both return 200 and are readable). This is a real org-level API-access policy, not a token-scope limitation, and it blocked over a week of direct verification of oramasys/perpetua-core PR #1 despite the repo being open-source. The actual workaround, found only this week: plain web_fetch against ordinary github.com web pages (org profile, /pulls list, individual /pull/N conversation pages) is NOT blocked by this policy at all -- it returns full page content including PR descriptions, commit lists, and inline CodeRabbit review comments. This was a genuine gap in approach across many prior sessions/turns, not a true access wall: the API was tried exclusively and repeatedly declared \"still blocked\" without ever testing whether a different tool (plain HTTP page fetch vs. REST API) could see around the same org policy. Confirmed directly via this method: oramasys/perpetua-core PR #1 (\"refactor(graph): final MiniGraph reconciliation\", diazMelgarejo, opened Aug 26 2026) is real, open, mergeable, currently at head commit 876a4581f1016735ec3bf1c1475cd11cc76a5bab (12 commits), with baseline main@8c063f4 recording 62 passing tests before this PR\\'s changes. As of two independent checks this week (both returning identical head SHA, confirming no drift), two CodeRabbit review findings remain genuinely open and unfixed on that real PR: (1) PerpetuaState.merge()\\'s more precise delta-aliasing bug -- model_copy(deep=True) deep-copies the EXISTING model but applies update=delta afterward without deep-copying delta\\'s own values, so a caller-held mutable reference passed in delta still aliases across state generations; orama-system\\'s own docs (01-kernel-spec.md) were corrected to the complete fix (model_copy(update=copy.deepcopy(delta), deep=True), verified against all three isolation properties directly) but this correction has not yet been ported back into the real perpetua-core code as of this check; (2) .github/workflows/test.yml\\'s actions/checkout@v4 step does not set persist-credentials: false, flagged CWE-522 by zizmor SAST -- pull-request-controlled test code can access the persisted contents token, unfixed as of this check.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_230c6d"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-28T22:42:22.399226+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-29T21:31:54.764214+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:e4c4c96a3f5d", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson e4c4c96a3f5d via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Two real security bugs found and fixed in diazMelgarejo/Claude-Desktop-LLM PR #1 (branch 2026-08-29-canonical-typescript-modernization, commits cb082af and 9a8c063), both worth auditing for in any v2/oramasys-org port of the same patterns, since this repo is TypeScript and forking into oramasys/* will involve re-deriving or re-porting this logic again.\\n\\n(1) src/tools/handlers.ts\\'s selectProvider() treated an omitted provider and an invalid supplied provider identically -- both silently fell through to ctx.config.activeProvider, so { provider: \"invalid\" } on any provider-selecting tool (e.g. local_llm_query) ran against activeProvider instead of erroring. Reachable despite the MCP tool JSON schemas declaring provider: { enum: [\"ollama\",\"lmstudio\"] } for every such tool -- schema hints describe intent to well-behaved clients but the server had no independent enforcement between receiving a raw tool-call request and dispatching to handleToolCall. Fixed with one validation gate at the shared call site before selectProvider, covering every provider-selecting tool through the same switch statement. General lesson for any MCP tool server: never treat a declared JSON-schema enum as the actual enforcement boundary -- validate server-side independently, at the one shared dispatch point if the codebase has one, not per-tool.\\n\\n(2) src/policy/endpoint-policy.ts\\'s validateAndPin() computed addressIsLoopback = isLoopbackAddress(resolvedIp) || isLoopbackHostname(hostname) and skipped ALL of ALLOW_REMOTE_LLM/ALLOWED_LLM_HOSTS/HTTPS enforcement whenever this was true -- meaning a hostname that merely resolves to 127.0.0.1 via DNS got FULL loopback trust, identical to a hostname the caller directly wrote as \"localhost\" or \"127.0.0.1\". Concretely exploitable: an attacker who controls DNS for their own domain (they always do, for domains they own) can point it at 127.0.0.1 and get complete bypass of every remote-endpoint protection with zero allowlisting and zero HTTPS requirement, because the connection is pinned to the resolved IP (closing the classic DNS-rebinding TOCTOU gap) but the original attacker-chosen hostname is preserved for the Host header/TLS SNI -- this is a real security-boundary bypass, not cosmetic. There is no legitimate case where a genuinely-remote, allowlisted, HTTPS endpoint should ever resolve to loopback. Fixed with isDirectLoopbackSpecification(): true only for a recognized loopback name or a literal loopback IP the caller wrote directly, never based on a DNS lookup result; a hostname that is not itself loopback but resolves there is now rejected unconditionally, before any allow/allowlist/HTTPS check can permit it.\\n\\nThe comparative architecture finding, more consequential than either individual fix: this TypeScript file\\'s own doc comment says it \"mirrors Perpetua-Tools\\' src/utils/ssrf_pinned_adapter.py,\" but checking that actual Python file (and its SSOT deny predicate in src/utils/ssrf_fetch_policy.py) shows a fundamentally different, more robust design -- _is_denied_ip checks addr.is_loopback (Python\\'s own ipaddress module property) on the ACTUAL RESOLVED address as one of several uniformly-denied ranges (loopback/private/link-local/CGNAT/multicast/reserved), with an explicit, separate allowlist mechanism layered on top for legitimate local-LLM use, and has NO hostname-string-based shortcut anywhere. The TypeScript port introduced a hostname-string classification path (isLoopbackHostname checking for \"localhost\"/\"*.localhost\") that grants automatic trust BEFORE uniformly checking the resolved address\\'s actual range, and that architectural divergence from the original pattern is exactly where this vulnerability was introduced during the port. Bulk-remediation implication for v2: when forking or re-deriving this pattern into oramasys/* repos, audit for this specific divergence class -- \"does the port classify by resolved-address-range uniformly (safe), or does it grant early trust based on the hostname string before resolution (unsafe)\" -- in any language, not just re-copy the TypeScript file\\'s current logic and assume it matches the canonical Python pattern.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_e4c4c9"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-29T21:31:54.764214+00:00"]}
