{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T04:36:43.555174+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:15d8d3b390c8", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 15d8d3b390c8 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Before continuing work on a shared feature branch across sessions, always git fetch+pull (ff-only) first, even mid-task -- do not assume the remote state matches what you last pushed.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_15d8d3"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:43.555174+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T04:36:44.558275+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:f9911f5b8e57", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson f9911f5b8e57 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'When adding a new validation/deny check to an existing multi-branch security gate, insert it AFTER the pre-existing allow/deny decision, not before -- ordering changes which error code fires for already-covered cases and can silently break passing tests.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_f9911f"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:44.558275+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T04:36:45.072855+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:22093a47c729", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 22093a47c729 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A stale GITHUB_TOKEN environment variable silently overrides a valid gh/git keyring credential over HTTPS, producing '401 Bad credentials' or 'Invalid username or token' even when 'gh auth status' shows a correctly logged-in account. Fix: unset GITHUB_TOKEN before git push / gh api calls, and re-unset every time since shell env does not persist across tool-call boundaries in this harness.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_22093a"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:45.072855+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T04:36:45.633220+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:a8314abe1daa", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson a8314abe1daa via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Before hardcoding a new default model name in a provider config, verify it is actually pulled/loaded in the live runtime (e.g. curl the provider's own /api/tags or /v1/models) rather than assuming availability from a naming convention or another config file's reference to it.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_a8314a"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:45.633220+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T04:36:45.994061+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:ebe2c3995415", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson ebe2c3995415 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Promoting a personal-account repo (diazMelgarejo/<repo>) to 'canonical under oramasys' is done by fast-forward-pushing the oramasys GitHub fork's own main branch directly to the desired commit -- not by merging a PR from the personal repo into oramasys, and not by GitHub's repo-transfer feature. The personal repo and any open PRs against it are left untouched unless explicitly told to close/merge them.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_ebe2c3"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:45.994061+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T04:36:46.359569+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:ed9964a08154", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson ed9964a08154 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a user's instructions contain internally contradictory values (e.g. two different version-number formats for the same bump, or an ambiguous repo-location instruction), investigate the live/factual state first (check if referenced repos/branches actually exist, what state they're in) and THEN present AskUserQuestion with options grounded in those facts -- never guess a resolution or silently pick the 'most likely' reading.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_ed9964"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:46.359569+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T05:39:42.023844+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:d74d82b3b257", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson d74d82b3b257 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A custom undici connect.lookup callback must handle BOTH dns.lookup callback forms -- (err, address, family) AND, when options.all is true, (err, [{address, family}, ...]) -- because Node 22's net.Socket.connect requests Happy-Eyeballs-style lookups (options.all===true) for any HOSTNAME target. IP-literal targets never invoke connect.lookup at all, so tests using only IP literals will pass while every real hostname-based request throws 'Invalid IP address: undefined' in production.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_d74d82"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:39:42.023844+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T05:44:37.079631+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:fba170814ea7", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson fba170814ea7 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Ollama and LM Studio CAN run concurrently on this Mac for light-to-moderate single-request workloads -- the documented Mac inference-mode table (Ollama=active/warm primary, LM Studio=passive/probe-only) is an operational convention/division-of-labor choice, not a hard technical impossibility.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_fba170"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:44:37.079631+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T05:46:09.049780+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:d0beabdc9b21", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson d0beabdc9b21 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Retraction: lesson_fba170814ea7's 'Ollama and LM Studio can run concurrently' claim was vacuous and should not be trusted as evidence. check_llm_status only calls each provider's lightweight healthCheck() (a GET to /api/tags or /v1/models), which proves the HTTP server is reachable -- it does NOT prove a model is actually resident in GPU/unified memory at that instant. Ollama unloads idle models within minutes (confirmed via 'ollama ps' showing a model, then empty output shortly after); the two providers were almost certainly never actually memory-resident at the same moment during that test. The user explicitly stopped a follow-up test that would have force-loaded and concurrently hammered both engines, citing hardware/thermal risk -- do not attempt to settle this by deliberately loading concurrent heavy inference load on both engines; if the question matters, it needs a deliberate, cautious, user-directed test, not an agent-initiated stress test.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_d0beab"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:46:09.049780+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T05:51:39.282634+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:f60e9a3a7ade", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson f60e9a3a7ade via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"CLOSED, NEVER-REOPEN rule: NEVER run Ollama and LM Studio under real concurrent heavy inference load on this Mac (M2 Pro), and never design or run a test that would. This is not an open research question -- the user has direct first-hand experience of a prior agent doing exactly this and causing the laptop to overheat and force-shutdown, with no replacement available. Confirmed independently: M2 Pro units are documented to reach ~108C under sustained heavy load (8K video export case study), and Apple's MacBook Pro fan curves are tuned for quiet operation over aggressive cooling -- the chip runs very hot before throttling engages, which is exactly the profile that produces an emergency/force shutdown rather than a graceful slowdown. Two LLM engines holding large models in shared unified memory and sustaining heavy GPU load concurrently is precisely this failure mode. Superficial single-request 'both servers reachable' tests (e.g. calling each provider's lightweight healthCheck) are NOT evidence either way and must never be used to justify going further -- see the retraction in lesson_d0beabdc9b21.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_f60e9a"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:51:39.282634+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-01T04:04:30.368951+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:eb056e4ab737", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson eb056e4ab737 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'When fixing automated-review findings (CodeRabbit, etc.) across a multi-language port of the same logic (e.g. a Python core + TypeScript core implementing one contract), always check whether the SAME bug class exists in the sibling language/test file too, even if the review only flagged one side -- automated reviewers commonly miss the parallel copy.'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_eb056e"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:30.368951+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-01T04:04:30.792783+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:6bd737c6a512", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 6bd737c6a512 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"In a cross-language contract where a JSON/wire format represents 'value unknown' as null, a TypeScript guard written as `!== undefined` does NOT exclude null (null !== undefined is true in JS) -- it will fall through and get silently coerced (e.g. to 0 in a > comparison), producing a wrong decision. Use `!= null` (loose equality) to exclude both null and undefined in one check, or type the field explicitly as `T | null` and check that.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_6bd737"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:30.792783+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-01T04:04:31.167189+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:96854673cb96", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 96854673cb96 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"When a function has both a required-precondition check (e.g. 'signature must be non-empty') and an early-return branch for a different condition (e.g. 'decision is blocked, return early'), validate the precondition BEFORE the early-return branch, not after -- otherwise the early-return path silently skips the precondition entirely, and that's usually the path most likely to reach production unnoticed.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_968546"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:31.167189+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-01T04:04:31.550117+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:e73221f5a7b4", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson e73221f5a7b4 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"An async function's 'I performed side-effect X, so set flag claimed=True to remember to clean it up on cancellation' pattern is NOT cancellation-safe if the flag is set on the line AFTER an awaited call that can itself be cancelled mid-flight after its underlying effect already happened. The cancellation-safe window is the gap between 'effect happened' (inside the awaited call, invisible to the caller) and 'caller's own flag reflects it' (the next line) -- a CancelledError landing in that exact gap causes the caller's cleanup handler to see claimed=False and skip releasing a resource that was actually already acquired.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_e73221"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:31.550117+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-01T04:04:31.916236+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:5951daa6d4f8", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 5951daa6d4f8 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"To post to the PT/orama multi-agent coordination board (GossipBus), use orchestrator.gossip_bus.GossipBus().emit(event_type, payload) from an async context -- never a raw sqlite3 INSERT into the gossip table. emit() runs the payload through classify_and_redact() (privacy/governance filtering), handles event_uuid dedup via INSERT OR IGNORE, and schedules embedding for genuinely-new events. event_type is a Literal enum (dispatch/route/result/error/heartbeat/fleet_topology_*/split_brain_*) -- free-form agent status notes use event_type='heartbeat' with payload {'kind': 'agent_note', 'agent_id': ..., 'message': ...}, matching the one prior real-world usage found in the board's history. DB path: resolve via GossipBus()'s default (git-common-dir-relative .state/perpetua_core.db), not a hardcoded path.\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_5951da"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:31.916236+00:00"]}
