{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T02:00:31.028198+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:f6cbb9825f37", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson f6cbb9825f37 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'Choose the canonical representation before choosing a hash. Exact-byte hashing is correct for Git blobs, tracked files, and binary transport, where an exact byte identity is the actual invariant. It is wrong when applied to a mutable external text field (e.g. a GitHub PR/issue body) whose transport (JSON serialization, CLI output, shell command substitution) does not preserve incidental bytes such as a trailing newline -- comparing raw bytes there creates false-positive integrity failures on genuinely correct writes, not stronger integrity'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_f6cbb9"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-13T02:00:31.028198+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T02:00:40.168464+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:b641aa5bc682", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson b641aa5bc682 via .agent/tools/learn.py: 'A new regression test passing is not TDD GREEN by itself -- run the whole neighboring test file, not just the new test, since a fix aimed at one failure mode can silently break an established, adjacent positive invariant it never re-checked'", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_b641aa"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-13T02:00:40.168464+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T02:00:48.951795+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:b1377bbce2a0", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson b1377bbce2a0 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"Do not resolve a review thread involving executable behavior until the exact current head is confirmed green on the affected tests and relevant CI -- a resolved thread's UI state is not proof of correctness, and describing planned validation as if it were completed evidence is a distinct, separate error from the underlying bug\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_b1377b"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-13T02:00:48.951795+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T02:00:58.737919+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:32c529e8de3c", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 32c529e8de3c via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"An automated code review finding is scoped to the exact commit range the reviewer actually examined, not to submission time -- when a later fix appears to contradict an earlier review comment, check the reviewer's own reviewed commit range against the current head before implementing the older recommendation literally, since the finding may already be stale\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_32c529"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-13T02:00:58.737919+00:00"]}
{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T02:01:12.071352+00:00", "skill": "learn", "action": "manual-stage:10568fa659b1", "result": "success", "detail": "Manually staged lesson 10568fa659b1 via .agent/tools/learn.py: \"A dependency needed only to exercise cross-package compatibility in CI should not be published through any package metadata at all -- not project.dependencies, and not any [project.optional-dependencies] extra, since an optional extra is still published metadata that advertises a dependency relationship the package does not actually own. Install the compatibility fixture from a separate requirements file in a distinct CI step instead, keeping the package's own dependency declarations completely free of it\"", "pain_score": 1, "importance": 6, "reflection": "", "confidence": 0.9, "source": {"skill": "learn", "profile": "manual", "run_id": "manual_10568f"}, "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-13T02:01:12.071352+00:00"]}
